## Documents Bot – Proposed Project Type and Communication

### Bot project type

The proposed Documents Bot should be a **Windows Forms App using modern .NET**, running as a **system-tray application** for each signed-in Windows user.

Although it is a WinForms project, it should use the .NET Generic Host and background services internally for:

- Monitoring local folders
- Processing upload and download queues
- Communicating with the Corto backend
- Receiving commands from the PWA and COM add-in
- Running periodic folder reconciliation

A WinForms tray application is recommended instead of a Windows Service because the Bot needs to interact with the signed-in user, open folder pickers, access user folders, communicate with Office applications and display synchronization status.

The Bot can use modern .NET even if the existing COM add-in uses .NET Framework because they will communicate through a defined message contract rather than referencing each other’s code directly.

### Main app/PWA communication with the Bot

The PWA should communicate with the locally installed Bot through a **secure localhost API**.

Proposed communication channels:

- **HTTPS REST API** for commands and status checks
- **WebSocket** for real-time synchronization progress and notifications
- **Custom URL protocol** such as `corto-documents://` to start or activate the Bot

Example PWA-to-Bot commands include:

- Check whether the Bot is installed and running
- Start a matter-folder import
- Open the native Windows folder picker
- Start, pause or cancel synchronization
- Request current synchronization status

The Bot can send real-time events back to the PWA, including:

- Folder selected
- Synchronization started
- Upload/download progress
- Synchronization completed
- Conflict detected
- Authentication or connection error

The localhost API must validate the Corto website origin and require a short-lived authenticated token. A technical POC should confirm that this approach works with the supported Edge/Chrome versions and company browser policies.

The Bot should also maintain an authenticated **SignalR connection to the Corto backend** to receive remote document changes and support multi-user synchronization. Backend SignalR can also be used as a fallback if direct PWA-to-localhost communication is restricted.

### COM add-in communication with the Bot

The Word and Outlook COM add-in should communicate with the Bot using **Windows Named Pipes**.

Named Pipes are recommended because:

- Both applications run locally on the same Windows computer.
- No network port needs to be exposed.
- Access can be restricted to the current Windows user.
- They work between a .NET Framework COM add-in and a modern .NET Bot.
- They support fast request-and-response communication.

The COM add-in can send a `documentLocation` message containing information such as:

- Account ID
- Matter ID
- Local document path
- Source application, such as Word or Outlook
- Command ID
- Timestamp

The Bot should immediately acknowledge that the command has been accepted and stored in its local queue. The Bot—not the COM add-in—will then validate the matter, monitor the file, upload it and handle retries or conflicts.

If the Bot is not running, the COM add-in should start the installed Bot and retry the connection.

### Proposed communication summary

| Component | Communication method | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| PWA → Bot | Local HTTPS REST API | Commands, health checks and status requests |
| Bot → PWA | Local WebSocket | Live progress, completion and conflict notifications |
| PWA → Bot | Custom URL protocol | Start or activate the Bot |
| COM add-in ↔ Bot | Windows Named Pipes | Pass local document information and receive status |
| Bot ↔ Corto backend | HTTPS API and SignalR | Authentication, document operations and remote-change notifications |

This keeps local filesystem access inside the Bot while the PWA and COM add-in only send commands and receive status updates.
